<p class='text'>I did not go and see the David Hockney exhibition "Bigger and Closer".</p>
<p class='text'>Even though I didn't go, I did see all the marketing for it around Manchester, and I received the emails and the like.  In deciding not to go, I read  <a href='https://factoryinternational.org/whats-on/david-hockney-bigger-and-closer-not-smaller-and-further-away/'>the blurb for it</a>, and I was taken aback (in a good way), by the following assertion he made:</p>
<p class='text'>$The world is very very beautiful if you look at it, but most people don’t look very much. They scan the ground in front of them so they can walk, they don’t really look at things incredibly well, with an intensity. I do.$</p>
<p class='text'>To be clear, it's the "I do" that took me aback.  If you think about it (I did), it's a very powerful claim.  He is not claiming to be unique, nor is he claiming to have any special skill or talent that the rest of us do not have, simply that, on a daily basis, he looks at things <i>that the rest of us do not</i>.</p>
<p class='text'>I remember many years ago being told that "nobody ever looks up" and, having been told that, felt that I needed to challenge this, by, naturally, looking up.  When I did this, I was staggered by the architecture and the buildings that are all around us.  If you walk along your local high street, or in any city centre, what you <i>notice</i>, what stands out to you, are the shop fronts and office entrances.  But when you look up, almost all of them are just the ground floor of much bigger buildings, some of which are genuinely beautiful.  Try it sometime.  Look up.</p>
<p class='text'>None of this is to claim that I look at things incredibly well, or with intensity, or that I notice details.  I don't.  If you ask me about just about any of the details of my route from my flat to Manchester Central Library (which I walked less than an hour ago), I could tell you very little.  Today, there is a queue outside the library because Oasis are playing at Heaton Park and they are selling merchandise in St Peter's Square.  I had to walk through that queue to get in.  But no, I couldn't tell you what one person was wearing.  I couldn't even tell you (roughly) how many people were in the queue, or how many of them were male or female.  In short, other than the <i>fact</i> that there was a queue, I could tell you nothing about it.</p>
<p class='text'>But at the same time, I would make <i>different</i> but nevertheless similiarly arrogant claims for myself.  And that made me wonder: is the same true for everybody, whether they know it or not?  Are a lot of the problems we have in the world simply that other people are just not as sensitive to certain things as we are (but are more sensitive to other things)?</p>
<p class='text'>Here are two assertions I will make about myself: firstly, that most people do not think deeply about things - the things they do, why they do them, how they do them, what they mean - but I do; secondly, that most people do not look at a list of things and immediately - and effortlessly - prioritize them: I do.</p>
<p class='text'>If you look at either of those statements and say "well, I do too", I am not inherently invalidating your lived experience: but ask yourself, does it frustrate you on a daily basis that other people do not do the same?  If so, please get in touch; I'm sure we'll be firm friends: I am with everyone else I've met who has these two same qualities.  Otherwise, I think you come into the same category I am in with regard to David Hockney's assertion: yes, I know <i>how</i> to look up, but no, I don't do it very often or with intensity.</p>
<p class='text'>There is a sense in which the very existence of this website - and certainly a lot of the content on it - reflects the fact that over the years there have been so many things that I have thought and wanted to say to the world at large, things I have often thought deeply about and come up with unusual answers, but that the world "does not want to hear".  This gives me the opportunity to say these things without worrying about whether anybody is even listening.</p>
<p class='text'>So, what arrogant assertion would you make about yourself?  What do you see yourself doing on a daily basis that <i>nobody else seems to care about</i>?</p>
